fund-csharp/23-authentication-authorizat.explanation:
Challenge early, run everything, then combine:

```csharp
var authenticated = user.Identity?.IsAuthenticated == true;
if (policy.RequireAuthenticatedUser && !authenticated) return Decision.Challenge;
var context = new AuthContext(user, resource);
foreach (var requirement in policy.Requirements)
    foreach (var handler in handlers) handler.Handle(context, requirement);
if (!context.Failed && policy.Requirements.All(context.HasSucceeded)) return Decision.Allow;
return authenticated ? Decision.Forbid : Decision.Challenge;
```

The tests catch the usual wrong evaluators: `Any` instead of `All` over the requirements (the role alone gets in), ignoring `Failed` (the suspended user gets in), always answering `Forbid` on a failure (the anonymous caller should be challenged), and treating "has claims" or `user.Identity != null` as signed in (the identity without an authentication type).

